/*
  AI Workshop: workshop-day content
  ---------------------------------
  What participants see on the "Workshop day" page: every block of the full-day HR and CX
  workshops in running order (8:30 a.m. to 4:30 p.m.), with the scenario, the practice
  material, ready-to-copy prompts and the workshop skills to download. Answers are
  deliberately NOT here: several exercises contain planted mistakes for participants to
  find. Answers live in the facilitator guide and activity cards.

  Fields per block
    id       short unique name
    time     clock time the block starts
    title    what the participant sees
    minutes  length of the block
    step     the Claude feature or habit it teaches
    intro    one or two sentences on what to do
    parts    list of {h: heading, text: material to read, prompt: text to copy,
                      files: [{href, label}] downloads}
    alt      optional alternate task (HR staff who don't recruit)

  Everything here is made up. Never replace it with real staff or customer data.
*/
window.WORKSHOPS = {

/* ======================= Human Resources ======================= */
HR: {
  title: "Filling a vacancy",
  scenario: "A Customer Service Representative post in the contact centre is vacant. Through the day you take it from the manager's three-line brief to the new hire's first week. Everything is made up: the manager is \"Supervisor B\" and the applicants are Applicant A, B and C.",
  blocks: [
  { id: "hr-login", time: "8:30", title: "Arrive and log in", minutes: 15, step: "Chat", intro: "Open claude.ai on your work laptop and sign in with your company account, not a personal one. Then send the message below. Show a facilitator when it works. If you can't get in, tell the floater now.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Your first message","prompt":"Hi Claude. I work in Human Resources at a Jamaican utility company. In 3 short bullet points, tell me which everyday tasks in my kind of role you could help with."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-m1", time: "8:45", title: "Module 1: Meet Claude", minutes: 45, step: "Claude 101, lessons 1 and 2", intro: "What Claude is, what it can see, and your first real conversations. 10 minutes of teaching, a 5-minute sorting game, then Claude Quest.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"1. Claude is a thinking partner, not a search box. It drafts, summarises, analyses, explains and reasons; you bring the context and the judgement.\n2. Claude writes the most likely next words. Fluent is not the same as true: give it the source and it can quote it.\n3. It only knows what is in front of it: this chat, files you attach, your Project, and its memory of you. It cannot see JPS systems unless they are connected.\n4. Company account: chats are not used to train models by default. Don't give work chats a thumbs up or down, because that sends the chat to Anthropic as feedback. The data rules still apply.\n5. Personalise it: Settings, then Instructions, for preferences that apply to every chat; Settings, then Memory, to see what Claude remembers.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/what-is-claude","label":"Claude Academy: What is Claude?"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/your-first-conversation-with-claude","label":"Claude Academy: Your first conversation with Claude"}]},
      {"h":"Game: search box or thinking partner? (5 min)","text":"For each request, decide as a table: could a search box answer it, or does it need Claude as a thinking partner?\n1. \"What is Jamaica's current minimum wage?\"\n2. \"Help me reply to a staff member who is upset that their leave was refused.\"\n3. \"What date is Heroes Day this year?\"\n4. \"Could anything in this job advert put older applicants off? [advert pasted]\"\n5. \"Turn my rough interview notes into a fair, balanced summary.\"\n6. \"What does TRN stand for?\"\nThe rule: if the answer depends on your situation, your draft or your judgement, it needs a thinking partner."},
      {"h":"Claude Quest (25 min, teams)","text":"Do as many cards as you can and show a facilitator the result on screen. Use only the practice files and made-up details."},
      {"h":"Level 1 (1 point each)","text":"1. Name it: start a new chat, ask anything, then rename the chat \"Quest\".\n2. Read a file: attach practice/HR_Leave_Policy_Extract.txt and ask for the 3 most important facts, quoting the line each comes from.\n3. Fix it, don't fight it: send \"write something about leave\", then edit that same message into a five-part prompt and send it again. Compare the two answers.\n4. Two versions: ask for a 2-sentence welcome message for new staff, then ask for a different version. Pick the better one and say why.","files":[{"href":"practice/HR_Leave_Policy_Extract.txt","label":"HR_Leave_Policy_Extract.txt"}]},
      {"h":"Level 2 (2 points each)","text":"5. Read a photo: upload practice/Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg and use the prompt below. Then check every digit against the photo yourself.\n6. Whiteboard to actions: upload practice/Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg and use the prompt below. Did Claude invent anything the board doesn't say?\n7. Get a spreadsheet: Create an Excel leave tracker for 5 made-up staff (Staff A to Staff E) with columns: Name, Unused days, Days carried over (maximum 5), Days lost. Fill in sample numbers and use formulas for the last two columns. Open the file.\n8. Get a Word document: Create an interview invitation for the Customer Service Representative role as a Word document. Use placeholders such as [Name], [Date], [Time] and [Location] for every detail. Open the file.","files":[{"href":"practice/Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg","label":"Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg"},{"href":"practice/Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg","label":"Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg"}]},
      {"h":"Card 5 prompt","prompt":"What is the meter reading in this photo? Give each digit separately and tell me how sure you are about each one."},
      {"h":"Card 6 prompt","prompt":"Turn this whiteboard into an action list table with the columns Action, Owner and Due. Write \"not stated\" wherever the board doesn't say. Don't guess."},
      {"h":"Bonus cards, if you finish (3 points each)","text":"9. Search with sources: ask Claude to search the web for when Jamaica's Data Protection Act came into effect, and whether all of it is enforced yet, with links. Open at least two links: do they say the same thing?\n10. Voice: in the Claude phone app, use voice mode to ask for 3 tips for staying calm on a difficult conversation with an employee.\n11. Make a mini tool: ask Claude for a simple interactive checklist you can tick off, for a new hire's first day. Click through it."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-m2", time: "9:30", title: "Module 2: Description (prompting that works)", minutes: 45, step: "AI Fluency: Description", intro: "How to tell Claude what you want. 15 minutes of teaching, then the Bad Prompt Makeover.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"The 4D framework","text":"Today is built on Anthropic's AI Fluency framework, four skills for working with AI:\nDelegation: deciding what Claude does and what you do.\nDescription: telling Claude clearly what you want.\nDiscernment: judging what Claude gives back.\nDiligence: using it responsibly and owning the result.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","label":"Claude Academy: AI Fluency: Framework and foundations"}]},
      {"h":"Every prompt: stage, task, rules","text":"Set the stage: who you are, your goal, and the context Claude needs.\nDefine the task: what Claude should do (write, analyse, compare, plan).\nSpecify the rules: format, length, tone, and an example if you have one.\nThe five parts you learned (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) fit inside these three."},
      {"h":"Six techniques, plus the secret weapon","text":"1. Give context: what you want, why, and who it is for.\n2. Show examples of what good looks like.\n3. Specify constraints: format, length, what to leave out.\n4. Break complex tasks into steps.\n5. Ask Claude to think first, before it answers.\n6. Define the role or tone.\nSecret weapon: ask Claude to help write the prompt.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/effective-prompting-techniques","label":"Claude Academy: Effective prompting techniques"}]},
      {"h":"Secret weapon prompt","prompt":"I'm trying to get you to help me with [goal]. I'm not sure how to phrase my request to get the best results. Ask me what you need to know, then write the prompt for me."},
      {"h":"Bad Prompt Makeover (30 min, teams)","text":"Each team takes two of these weak prompts:\n1. \"Write a job ad.\"\n2. \"Summarise this policy.\"\n3. \"Write an email about the training day.\"\n4. \"Give me interview questions.\"\nFor each one:\n1. Run it as it is in a new chat, with made-up details where needed. Note what is wrong with the answer.\n2. Rewrite it using stage, task, rules and at least three of the six techniques.\n3. Run the new version in a new chat and compare.\n4. Put your best before and after on the projector. The room votes for the best makeover."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-break1", time: "10:15", title: "Break", minutes: 15, step: "", intro: "Back on time, please: the next block starts promptly.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "hr-m3", time: "10:30", title: "Module 3: Discernment (checking what Claude gives you)", minutes: 30, step: "AI Fluency: Discernment", intro: "How to judge an answer and improve it. 5 minutes of teaching, then two drills: find what the AI made up, then decide what may go into your company account.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Judge three things: the product (is it accurate and useful?), the process (did Claude go about it sensibly?) and the performance (is it working with you the way you need?).\nThen loop: describe, check, refine. Treat a first draft as a starting point.\nGive specific feedback (\"cut the first two paragraphs and end with the action\"), not just \"make it better\".\nIf a chat goes off track, start a new chat with a clearer prompt.\nFor anything that matters, check the facts yourself and ask Claude to cite its source.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/getting-better-results","label":"Claude Academy: Getting better results"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/the-description-discernment-loop","label":"Claude Academy: The Description-Discernment loop"}]},
      {"h":"Useful follow-ups","prompt":"How confident are you in each part of this answer? List anything I should double-check, and where I would check it."},
      {"h":"A. spot the made-up rule","text":"An AI tool wrote the answer below. Two statements in it are not in the policy. Find them with your partner before you use the AI."},
      {"h":"Policy extract","text":"Employees may carry over up to 5 unused vacation days into the next calendar year. Carried-over days must be used by 31 March. Days above 5 are forfeited unless a manager approves an exception in writing."},
      {"h":"Question","text":"\"Hi, I still have 8 vacation days left. Will I lose them in January?\""},
      {"h":"AI answer","text":"Hi, thanks for checking. Good news: you can carry over up to 5 of your 8 days into next year, and you don't need to do anything, as carry-over happens automatically. Just remember to use them by 31 March. The other 3 days will be paid out with your January salary. If you need to keep more than 5, ask your manager to approve an exception in writing. Kind regards, HR"},
      {"h":"Step 2: paste all three into Claude, then send","prompt":"Check the answer above against the policy extract only. List every statement in the answer that the policy does not support, and say what the policy actually says. Do not use any outside knowledge."},
      {"h":"B. Data rules","text":"Part A without the AI: sort each item into Share, Anonymise first or Never share. Part B: rewrite both texts so they could go into an AI tool and still do their job. You remove the details yourself, before anything is pasted."},
      {"h":"Part A: sort these","text":"The leave policy · A draft job advert · A CV · Staff survey comments with names removed · The salary scale for a role · A medical certificate · A disciplinary letter · Interview questions · A payroll export"},
      {"h":"Part B: CV extract (made up)","text":"Tanya-Marie Richards · TRN 123-456-789 · 14 Sample Avenue, Kingston · 876-555-0142 · born 12 April 1996. Customer Service Agent at a telecoms company, 2021 to 2024: handled billing and fault calls, top agent for customer satisfaction in 2023."},
      {"h":"Part B: leave email (made up)","text":"Hi, it's Marlon Brown in Billing, employee no. 40217. My doctor says I need knee surgery next month, so I need three weeks off from 5 to 23 May. How much sick leave do I have left?"}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-m4", time: "11:00", title: "Module 4: Projects: build the HR Policy Desk", minutes: 15, intro: "Set up a Claude Project once and every chat inside it answers from your documents. In Claude: Projects, then Create project, named HR Policy Desk. Add practice/Sample_Hybrid_Work_Policy.docx and practice/HR_Leave_Policy_Extract.txt, then paste the instructions below.", step: "Claude 101, lesson 5",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"A Project is a workspace for ongoing work: its instructions and files apply to every chat inside it, so you never re-explain.\nUse one when you keep re-uploading the same documents, or when answers must always follow the same rules.\nName files clearly: Claude uses file names to find the right information.\nOn company plans you can share a Project with colleagues (view or edit), so the team works from one source.\nProjects store knowledge. Skills, in Module 6, store a way of doing a task.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/introduction-to-projects","label":"Claude Academy: Introduction to projects"}]},
      {"h":"Project instructions","prompt":"You are an HR assistant at a Jamaican utility company. Answer only from the documents in this project. Give the section number for every answer. If the documents do not answer a question, say \"The policy doesn't say\" and list the question for HR to confirm. Never guess about pay, leave balances or eligibility. Write in plain, friendly English."},
      {"h":"Test 1 (new chat in the project)","prompt":"How many vacation days can I carry over?"},
      {"h":"Test 2","prompt":"Is there a gym allowance?"}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-ex1", time: "11:15", title: "1. Job description and advert", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Supervisor B sends HR three lines about the vacancy.", step: "Working in your Project",
    parts: [
      {"h":"The brief","text":"Need 1 CSR for the contact centre. Handles calls, email and WhatsApp about bills, outages and new connections. Must stay calm with upset customers and be good on a computer. Shift work including some weekends. CXC English and Maths minimum; call centre experience a plus."},
      {"h":"Step 1: job description","prompt":"Role: You are an HR recruitment specialist at a large Jamaican utility company.\nTask: Turn the manager's brief below into a full job description.\nContext: The brief is: \"Need 1 CSR for the contact centre. Handles calls, email and WhatsApp about bills, outages and new connections. Must stay calm with upset customers and be good on a computer. Shift work including some weekends. CXC English and Maths minimum; call centre experience a plus.\"\nFormat: Use these headings: Job title, Purpose of the role, Key responsibilities (6 to 8 bullets), Minimum qualifications, Preferred experience, Skills and attributes, Working hours, Reports to. Plain, inclusive language.\nCheck: Do not invent a salary, benefits or location. Mark anything you had to assume with [CHECK]. After the job description, list the questions I should ask the manager before it is published."},
      {"h":"Step 2: advert (same chat)","prompt":"Now turn this job description into a job advert for social media and the careers page. Under 150 words, warm and direct, written to the reader (\"you\"). End with how to apply, using [Closing date] and [Application link] as placeholders. Keep every [CHECK] item out of the advert."},
      {"h":"Step 3: make the file (same chat)","prompt":"Create this job description as a Word document with the same headings."}
    ],
    alt: {"h":"Not in recruitment: training outline","text":"Brief: \"Need a half-day refresher for contact centre staff on handling billing disputes. Most are new. Must cover tone and the estimated-bill process.\"","prompt":"Role: You are a learning and development officer at a Jamaican utility.\nTask: Turn the manager's brief below into a half-day training outline.\nContext: [paste the brief]\nFormat: 3 to 5 learning outcomes, then a timed agenda as a table, then one practice activity.\nCheck: Mark any assumption with [CHECK]. Do not invent company procedures; leave [PROCEDURE] where one is needed."} },
  { id: "hr-ex2", time: "11:35", title: "2. Screening three CVs", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. The panel wants the same summary of each application. The AI lays out evidence; the panel decides.", step: "Uploading files; people decide",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Criteria","text":"1. CXC passes in English and Maths\n2. Experience dealing with customers\n3. Comfortable using computer systems\n4. Available for shift work, including weekends\n5. Preferred: call centre experience"},
      {"h":"Applicant A","text":"CXC: English Grade II, Mathematics Grade III, Office Administration Grade I. Customer Service Agent, telecoms call centre, 2021 to 2024: billing and fault calls by phone and live chat; logged cases in a CRM ticketing system; highest customer satisfaction score on the team in 2023. Available for rotating shifts."},
      {"h":"Applicant B","text":"CXC: English Grade I, Principles of Business Grade II. Cashier, supermarket, 2020 to 2024: handled payments and returns, trained new cashiers. Volunteer, church office: data entry in Excel. Would prefer weekday work."},
      {"h":"Applicant C","text":"Associate degree in Business Administration, 2023. CXC: English Grade I, Mathematics Grade II. Administrative assistant, law office, 2023 to now: answers phones, schedules appointments, uses Word and Outlook daily."},
      {"h":"Prompt","prompt":"Role: You are helping an HR shortlisting panel. You lay out evidence; you do not make decisions.\nTask: Compare each applicant below against the five criteria.\nContext: [paste the criteria and the three CVs]\nFormat: One table per applicant with columns Criterion, Rating (Met, Partly met or Not shown) and Evidence (quoted from the CV).\nCheck: Use only what the CV says. Do not rank the applicants, give an overall score or recommend anyone. List what the panel should ask each applicant at interview to fill the gaps."},
      {"h":"Part 2: another AI tool wrote this about Applicant B. Find two errors.","text":"Applicant B. 1. English and Maths: Met (CXC English Grade I, Mathematics Grade II). 2. Customers: Met (cashier). 3. Computer systems: Partly met (Excel). 4. Shifts: Partly met (prefers weekdays). 5. Call centre: Partly met (some call centre experience)."}
    ],
    alt: {"h":"Not in recruitment: compare training proposals","text":"Criteria: under $160,000; on site; certificate on completion; 2 days or less; references from another utility.\nProvider A: 2 days, on site, certificate, $180,000.\nProvider B: 1 day online, recordings included, no certificate, $90,000.\nProvider C: 2 days, on site, certificate, $150,000, references from two utilities.\nUse the screening prompt above, with \"proposal\" in place of \"CV\"."} },
  { id: "hr-ex5", time: "11:55", title: "5. Questions from the new hire", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Everyone does this one, inside your HR Policy Desk (or attach practice/Sample_Hybrid_Work_Policy.docx to a normal chat). The new representative emails HR four questions.", step: "Answering from Project files",
    parts: [
      {"h":"The questions","text":"1. Can I work from home two days a week?\n2. Will the company pay for my internet if I work from home?\n3. Can I use ChatGPT at home to help me write customer emails?\n4. If hybrid work doesn't suit me, how do I stop?"},
      {"h":"Step 1: answer them","prompt":"Role: You are an HR officer answering a new employee.\nTask: Answer the four questions below.\nContext: The employee is a Customer Service Representative in the Contact Centre who started this week. Use only the attached hybrid work policy.\nFormat: One short answer per question, with the policy section number in brackets. Then a friendly reply email under 180 words that covers all four.\nCheck: If the policy does not clearly answer a question for this employee, say so and do not guess. List those questions separately for HR to confirm.\n[paste the four questions]"},
      {"h":"Step 2: staff FAQ (same chat)","prompt":"Now write an FAQ of the 8 questions staff are most likely to ask about this policy. Answer each in no more than 3 sentences, taken only from the policy, with the section number. Under a separate heading \"Questions to raise with HR leadership\", list common questions the policy does not answer."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-lunch", time: "12:15", title: "Lunch", minutes: 45, step: "", intro: "Back at 1:00 sharp.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "hr-m5", time: "1:00", title: "Module 5: Artifacts and files", minutes: 40, step: "Claude 101, lesson 6; built-in skills", intro: "Claude can make the finished thing, not just text in a chat. 5 minutes of teaching, then make two outputs from the morning's work and check them.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Artifacts are outputs that live in Claude: a doc, a deck, a design or a small tool. They open next to the chat, stay editable, and are shared by link. Ask for the deliverable: \"turn this into a one-page doc for the team\".\nClaude Docs, Claude Slides and Claude Design are the artifact types for documents, decks and visual designs, if JPS has them switched on.\nFiles are different: Claude makes a Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF file for you to download, using its built-in skills.\nEdit an artifact three ways: type in it yourself, comment for Claude, or ask in the chat.\nWhatever you make, open it and check it before it goes anywhere.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/creating-with-artifacts","label":"Claude Academy: Creating with artifacts"}]},
      {"h":"Option A: onboarding pack (Word)","prompt":"Create a Word document called \"New CSR Onboarding Pack\" for a new Customer Service Representative, with a cover page, a short welcome, a day-by-day first-week checklist with tick boxes, and a \"Who to ask\" table. Use placeholders for every name and date."},
      {"h":"Option B: interview scoring sheet (Excel)","prompt":"Create an Excel interview scoring sheet for the Customer Service Representative role: one row for each of six fair interview questions (write them with Claude first), and a score column from 1 to 5 for Applicant A, B and C. Add formulas for each applicant's total and average, and a second sheet explaining what each score means."},
      {"h":"Option C: panel briefing (PowerPoint)","prompt":"Create a 3-slide PowerPoint for the interview panel: 1) the role and the 5 criteria, 2) the interview plan and timings, 3) how to score fairly and what not to ask."},
      {"h":"Option D: a living doc (Claude Docs, if switched on)","prompt":"Create a first-week checklist for a new Customer Service Representative as a doc I can share with the team, with a short welcome at the top and a \"Who to ask\" table with placeholders for every name."},
      {"h":"Check every file","text":"Open it, don't just read Claude's reply. Tick each one:\n- Every fact matches your source\n- Placeholders are still in [brackets]: no invented names, dates or pay\n- Formulas work: change one score and the total should change\n- Nothing about age, family, religion or health"}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-m6", time: "1:40", title: "Module 6: Skills", minutes: 60, step: "Claude 101, lesson 7", intro: "A skill teaches Claude how to do one task your way, every time, in any chat. Projects store knowledge; skills store a way of working. Use one, read one, build one.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Skills are folders of instructions (and sometimes templates or scripts) that Claude loads when a task needs them.\nYou already use Anthropic's built-in skills whenever Claude makes a Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF file.\nThe easiest way to make your own is to ask Claude: it interviews you and builds the skill.\nThe description at the top of a skill is what tells Claude when to use it, so make it name the task plainly.\nOnly add skills from people you trust, and never put real names, accounts or cases in one.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/working-with-skills","label":"Claude Academy: Working with skills"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/tutorials/how-to-create-a-skill-with-claude-through-conversation","label":"Claude Academy: How to create a skill with Claude through conversation"}]},
      {"h":"1. Before and after (10 min): the HR letter writer","text":"If your account owner has added it for everyone, find it under Customize, then Skills, and switch it on. If not, download the ZIP file below, open your skills (Customize, then Skills) and use Add to upload it.\n\nTest A: switch the skill OFF, start a new chat, and send the prompt below.\nTest B: switch the skill ON, start a new chat, and send exactly the same thing.\nCompare: did each version use placeholders, avoid giving a reason, and list what HR must confirm?","files":[{"href":"skills/hr-letter-writer.zip","label":"Download hr-letter-writer.zip"}]},
      {"h":"Test prompt (A and B)","prompt":"Write a letter to the applicants we did not choose for the Customer Service Representative role."},
      {"h":"2. Look inside (10 min)","text":"Open the skill's instructions (link below). Find the four things every good skill has: a description saying when Claude should use it, the rules, the formats, and a check at the end. Which parts of a five-part prompt can you see? What would you add for your own team?","files":[{"href":"https://github.com/KennyCross/jps-ai-workshop/blob/main/skills/hr-letter-writer/SKILL.md","label":"Open SKILL.md"}]},
      {"h":"3. Build your own (25 min, pairs)","text":"Pick a task your team does the same way every week. Ideas: replies to leave questions that cite the policy section; an interview question bank for any role; a job advert from a manager's brief.\n1. Send the prompt below and answer Claude's questions.\n2. Claude builds the skill and shows you the skill file. Use its save button to add it to your skills. (No button? Download the file, open your skills and use Add to upload it.)\n3. Test it in a new chat with a made-up example. Open the steps Claude took: one should load your skill. If it doesn't, ask Claude to make the skill's description more specific, then save the new version.\nMade-up examples only inside a skill: never real names, accounts or cases."},
      {"h":"Build prompt","prompt":"I want to create a skill for this task: [your task]. Interview me one question at a time: how the task should be done, what must always be included, what must never happen, and what a good result looks like. Then build the skill."},
      {"h":"4. Swap (5 min)","text":"Try a neighbouring pair's skill (share it from your skills, or pass the file) and suggest one improvement."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-break2", time: "2:40", title: "Break", minutes: 15, step: "", intro: "Back on time, please: the next block starts promptly.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "hr-m7", time: "2:55", title: "Module 7: Connectors, enterprise search and Research", minutes: 25, step: "Claude 101, lessons 8 to 10", intro: "Claude reaching beyond the chat: your work tools, your organisation's knowledge and the web. Teaching and demonstrations, then a quick routing game.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Web search: a quick fact from current web pages, with links. On company plans an owner turns it on; in the newer Claude experience Claude searches when it helps.\nResearch: many searches that build on each other, then a cited report. Takes a few minutes or more. Start it from + in the message box.\nConnectors: Claude reaches tools such as Microsoft 365 (Outlook, SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams). It sees only what you can see. An owner adds a connector for the organisation, then you sign in with your own account.\nEnterprise search: answers from your organisation's own documents, chats and email, where it is set up.\nThinking: for a hard problem that needs reasoning, not information, choose a higher effort (click the model name, then Effort).","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/connecting-your-tools","label":"Claude Academy: Connecting your tools"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/research-mode-for-deep-dives","label":"Claude Academy: Research for deep dives"}]},
      {"h":"Game: which tool? (10 min)","text":"For each request, choose: web search, Research, a connector or enterprise search, thinking (higher effort), or a Project.\n1. \"What are Jamaica's public holidays in 2027?\"\n2. \"Compare what Caribbean employers include in their hybrid work policies, with sources.\"\n3. \"What did we decide about the December leave cut-off in last week's emails?\"\n4. \"Work out the fairest way to rota weekend shifts for eight people with these constraints.\"\n5. \"Answer staff questions from our leave policy and always give the section.\""},
      {"h":"Try web search (if it is on)","prompt":"What are Jamaica's public holidays in 2027? Give me the source link for each one."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-m8", time: "3:20", title: "Module 8: Delegation and Diligence: Pilot Lab", minutes: 45, step: "AI Fluency: Delegation and Diligence", intro: "Decide what Claude should do in one real weekly task, build it, test it, and take responsibility for it. Pitches start at 4:05.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Delegation","text":"Before you build, split the work: what does Claude do, what do you do, and what do you do together?\nThree ways to work with Claude: automation (Claude does a defined task), augmentation (you think and create together), agency (Claude works on its own within limits you set).\nDelegate the drafting, sorting and summarising. Keep the decisions about people, money and promises."},
      {"h":"Diligence","text":"Creation: use the company account and approved tools, and only data you are allowed to share.\nTransparency: tell people when Claude played a significant part in work that affects them.\nDeployment: you are responsible for what you send. Check it as if you wrote it yourself.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/a-closer-look-at-diligence","label":"Claude Academy: A closer look at Diligence"}]},
      {"h":"Chat, Project or skill?","text":"Chat: a one-off question or draft.\nProject: ongoing work that needs the same files and instructions, such as a policy desk.\nSkill: a task done the same way again and again, in any chat, such as a standard letter or update."},
      {"h":"Pilot plan (copy it and fill it in)","prompt":"PILOT PLAN\nTask:\nHow often we do it:\nTime it takes now:\nWe built a: Project / Skill, because:\nWhat Claude does:\nWhat a person always checks before it is used:\nData: what goes in, and what never goes in:\nHow we will know it works (one measure):\nWho needs to agree before we start:"},
      {"h":"Stress-test it","prompt":"Here are the instructions for my [Project / skill]: [paste them]. Act as a critical colleague. Give me 3 ways it could produce a wrong or risky answer, and a change to the instructions that would prevent each one."},
      {"h":"Diligence statement for your pilot","prompt":"Help me write a short diligence statement for this pilot: which AI tool we use, what Claude does in the task, what a person always checks before the work is used, and that we take responsibility for the final result."}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-pitch", time: "4:05", title: "Pilot pitches", minutes: 20, step: "Show what you built", intro: "Two minutes per pair, with what you built on the projector. Everyone votes for 2 pilots that aren't their own: the most useful, safe and ready to start on Monday. The top 3 go to management.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Your 2 minutes","text":"1. The task, and how often you do it\n2. What you built: show it working on a made-up example\n3. Before and after: time, quality, consistency\n4. What a person always checks\n5. How you will know it works"}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-close", time: "4:25", title: "Close", minutes: 5, step: "", intro: "Your Projects and skills stay in your Claude account. Write your commitment, fill in the feedback form, and keep learning on Claude Academy: the courses are free and end with a certificate.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"My commitment","prompt":"On Monday I will use Claude to ______, and before I use the answer I will check ______."},
      {"h":"Keep learning","text":"Claude 101: everything from today, in more depth.\nAI Fluency: Framework and foundations: the 4D framework.\nAI capabilities and limitations: why Claude gets things right and wrong.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101","label":"Claude Academy: Claude 101"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","label":"Claude Academy: AI Fluency"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-capabilities-and-limitations","label":"Claude Academy: AI capabilities and limitations"}]}
    ] },
  { id: "hr-ex3", time: "Extra", title: "Extra: 3. Interviews", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Part A builds a fair question bank. Part B is practice: one partner interviews, Claude plays Applicant B, the other partner watches. Ask at least 4 questions, including one about weekend shifts, then type END INTERVIEW.", step: "For fast pairs, or practice after the workshop",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Part A: question bank","prompt":"Role: You are an HR recruitment specialist.\nTask: Write a structured interview for the Customer Service Representative role, the same for every candidate.\nContext: [paste the job description from Exercise 1, or the five criteria from Exercise 2]\nFormat: 8 questions: 4 about past behaviour (\"Tell me about a time...\"), 2 about computer systems, 2 about handling customers. For each, give what a strong answer includes and one follow-up question.\nCheck: Every question must relate to the job. Flag any question that could touch on age, family, religion, health or other personal matters."},
      {"h":"Part B: mock interview (new chat)","prompt":"You are Applicant B in a job interview for a Customer Service Representative post at a utility company. Your background: CXC English Grade I and Principles of Business Grade II; cashier at a supermarket 2020 to 2024, where you handled payments and returns and trained new cashiers; volunteer data entry in Excel at a church office. You would prefer weekday work.\n\nStay in character. Answer the way a real candidate would: give some strong answers and some vague ones that need a follow-up. If you are asked about weekend work, mention that you have two young children. Do not give feedback until I type END INTERVIEW. Then step out of character and give me feedback on my interviewing: which questions were open or leading, whether I followed up on vague answers, and whether any question strayed from the job."}
    ],
    alt: {"h":"Not in recruitment: a difficult conversation (new chat)","prompt":"You are a staff member at a utility company. You are upset because your payslip shows a deduction you don't recognise. Stay in character and react like a real person: calmer if I listen and explain the process, more upset if I brush you off. Do not give feedback until I type END. Then step out of character and tell me whether I listened, explained the next step clearly, and avoided discussing amounts or other people's pay."} },
  { id: "hr-ex4", time: "Extra", title: "Extra: 4. Offer to first week", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. The panel has chosen. Write to the new hire and to the applicants who were not chosen. Each piece becomes a template.", step: "For fast pairs, or practice after the workshop",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Step 1: welcome pack","prompt":"Role: You are an HR onboarding coordinator at a Jamaican utility company.\nTask: Create a reusable welcome pack for a new Customer Service Representative.\nContext: They start at 8:00 a.m. on [Start date], report to [Supervisor] in the Contact Centre, and must bring photo ID, their TRN card and banking details for the payroll forms.\nFormat: 1) A welcome email under 150 words. 2) A day-by-day checklist for the first week covering IT setup, reading key policies, system training and shadowing a senior representative.\nCheck: Use [Name], [Start date] and [Supervisor] as placeholders; never invent names. List anything HR must confirm before sending."},
      {"h":"Step 2: the checklist as a file (same chat)","prompt":"Create the first-week checklist as a Word document with a tick box beside each item."},
      {"h":"Step 3: regret letter (same chat)","prompt":"Now write a short, kind letter to applicants who were not selected. Thank them, tell them clearly that they were not chosen this time, and invite them to apply for future roles. Do not give reasons for the decision. Use [Name] and [Role] as placeholders. Under 120 words."}
    ],
    alt: {"h":"Not in recruitment: benefits enrolment reminder","prompt":"Role: You are an HR benefits officer.\nTask: Write a reminder email about the health insurance open enrolment deadline, and a 5-question FAQ.\nContext: Deadline [Date]. Forms go to [Contact]. Changes take effect [Effective date].\nFormat: Email under 150 words; FAQ answers of no more than 2 sentences each.\nCheck: Use placeholders for every date, name and amount. Do not invent plan details; write [CONFIRM] instead."} }
  ]
},

/* ======================= Customer Experience ======================= */
CX: {
  title: "The week after a major outage",
  scenario: "A storm on Saturday night has caused a wide outage. Through the day you handle the week that follows. The internal outage note below is the only source of facts all week: if it isn't in the note, the AI must not say it. Everything is made up.",
  note: "INTERNAL – Outage update, Monday 9:00 a.m. A storm on Saturday night damaged lines in [Parish A] and [Parish B]. About 18,000 customers are without power. Crews are repairing main lines first, then local lines. [Parish A]: main areas expected back by Tuesday evening. [Parish B]: damage still being assessed; no restoration estimate yet. Fallen lines have been reported in several communities: treat every line as live and report it to [Hotline]. Meter reading is suspended this week, so some bills will be estimated and adjusted after the next actual reading.",
  blocks: [
  { id: "cx-login", time: "8:30", title: "Arrive and log in", minutes: 15, step: "Chat", intro: "Open claude.ai on your work laptop and sign in with your company account, not a personal one. Then send the message below. Show a facilitator when it works. If you can't get in, tell the floater now.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Your first message","prompt":"Hi Claude. I work in Customer Experience at a Jamaican utility company. In 3 short bullet points, tell me which everyday tasks in my kind of role you could help with."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-m1", time: "8:45", title: "Module 1: Meet Claude", minutes: 45, step: "Claude 101, lessons 1 and 2", intro: "What Claude is, what it can see, and your first real conversations. 10 minutes of teaching, a 5-minute sorting game, then Claude Quest.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"1. Claude is a thinking partner, not a search box. It drafts, summarises, analyses, explains and reasons; you bring the context and the judgement.\n2. Claude writes the most likely next words. Fluent is not the same as true: give it the source and it can quote it.\n3. It only knows what is in front of it: this chat, files you attach, your Project, and its memory of you. It cannot see JPS systems unless they are connected.\n4. Company account: chats are not used to train models by default. Don't give work chats a thumbs up or down, because that sends the chat to Anthropic as feedback. The data rules still apply.\n5. Personalise it: Settings, then Instructions, for preferences that apply to every chat; Settings, then Memory, to see what Claude remembers.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/what-is-claude","label":"Claude Academy: What is Claude?"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/your-first-conversation-with-claude","label":"Claude Academy: Your first conversation with Claude"}]},
      {"h":"Game: search box or thinking partner? (5 min)","text":"For each request, decide as a table: could a search box answer it, or does it need Claude as a thinking partner?\n1. \"How many characters fit in one SMS?\"\n2. \"Help me reply to a customer who has called four times about the same outage.\"\n3. \"When is the next public holiday?\"\n4. \"Which of these 40 comments are about billing? [comments pasted]\"\n5. \"Rewrite my reply so it sounds less defensive.\"\n6. \"What does kWh stand for?\"\nThe rule: if the answer depends on your situation, your draft or your judgement, it needs a thinking partner."},
      {"h":"Claude Quest (25 min, teams)","text":"Do as many cards as you can and show a facilitator the result on screen. Use only the practice files and made-up details."},
      {"h":"Level 1 (1 point each)","text":"1. Name it: start a new chat, ask anything, then rename the chat \"Quest\".\n2. Read a file: attach practice/CX_Outage_Note.txt and ask for the 3 most important facts, quoting the line each comes from.\n3. Fix it, don't fight it: send \"write a message about the outage\", then edit that same message into a five-part prompt and send it again. Compare the two answers.\n4. Two versions: ask for a 2-sentence welcome message for new staff, then ask for a different version. Pick the better one and say why.","files":[{"href":"practice/CX_Outage_Note.txt","label":"CX_Outage_Note.txt"}]},
      {"h":"Level 2 (2 points each)","text":"5. Read a photo: upload practice/Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg and use the prompt below. Then check every digit against the photo yourself.\n6. Whiteboard to actions: upload practice/Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg and use the prompt below. Did Claude invent anything the board doesn't say?\n7. Get a spreadsheet: Create an Excel sheet from the outage note with columns: Area, Status, Restoration estimate, Safety message. Write \"not in the note\" where the note gives nothing. Open the file.\n8. Get a Word document: Turn the outage note into a one-page customer notice as a Word document, with a clear heading, area-by-area status and the fallen-line safety message. Open the file.","files":[{"href":"practice/Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg","label":"Quest_Meter_Photo.jpg"},{"href":"practice/Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg","label":"Quest_Whiteboard_Photo.jpg"}]},
      {"h":"Card 5 prompt","prompt":"What is the meter reading in this photo? Give each digit separately and tell me how sure you are about each one."},
      {"h":"Card 6 prompt","prompt":"Turn this whiteboard into an action list table with the columns Action, Owner and Due. Write \"not stated\" wherever the board doesn't say. Don't guess."},
      {"h":"Bonus cards, if you finish (3 points each)","text":"9. Search with sources: ask Claude to search the web for when Jamaica's Data Protection Act came into effect, and whether all of it is enforced yet, with links. Open at least two links: do they say the same thing?\n10. Voice: in the Claude phone app, use voice mode to ask for 3 tips for staying calm on a difficult call with a customer.\n11. Make a mini tool: ask Claude for a simple interactive checklist you can tick off, for handling an outage call. Click through it."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-m2", time: "9:30", title: "Module 2: Description (prompting that works)", minutes: 45, step: "AI Fluency: Description", intro: "How to tell Claude what you want. 15 minutes of teaching, then the Bad Prompt Makeover.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"The 4D framework","text":"Today is built on Anthropic's AI Fluency framework, four skills for working with AI:\nDelegation: deciding what Claude does and what you do.\nDescription: telling Claude clearly what you want.\nDiscernment: judging what Claude gives back.\nDiligence: using it responsibly and owning the result.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","label":"Claude Academy: AI Fluency: Framework and foundations"}]},
      {"h":"Every prompt: stage, task, rules","text":"Set the stage: who you are, your goal, and the context Claude needs.\nDefine the task: what Claude should do (write, analyse, compare, plan).\nSpecify the rules: format, length, tone, and an example if you have one.\nThe five parts you learned (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) fit inside these three."},
      {"h":"Six techniques, plus the secret weapon","text":"1. Give context: what you want, why, and who it is for.\n2. Show examples of what good looks like.\n3. Specify constraints: format, length, what to leave out.\n4. Break complex tasks into steps.\n5. Ask Claude to think first, before it answers.\n6. Define the role or tone.\nSecret weapon: ask Claude to help write the prompt.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/effective-prompting-techniques","label":"Claude Academy: Effective prompting techniques"}]},
      {"h":"Secret weapon prompt","prompt":"I'm trying to get you to help me with [goal]. I'm not sure how to phrase my request to get the best results. Ask me what you need to know, then write the prompt for me."},
      {"h":"Bad Prompt Makeover (30 min, teams)","text":"Each team takes two of these weak prompts:\n1. \"Reply to this angry customer.\"\n2. \"Write an outage message.\"\n3. \"Summarise these complaints.\"\n4. \"Make this nicer.\"\nFor each one:\n1. Run it as it is in a new chat, with made-up details where needed. Note what is wrong with the answer.\n2. Rewrite it using stage, task, rules and at least three of the six techniques.\n3. Run the new version in a new chat and compare.\n4. Put your best before and after on the projector. The room votes for the best makeover."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-break1", time: "10:15", title: "Break", minutes: 15, step: "", intro: "Back on time, please: the next block starts promptly.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "cx-m3", time: "10:30", title: "Module 3: Discernment (checking what Claude gives you)", minutes: 30, step: "AI Fluency: Discernment", intro: "How to judge an answer and improve it. 5 minutes of teaching, then two drills: find what the AI made up, then decide what may go into your company account.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Judge three things: the product (is it accurate and useful?), the process (did Claude go about it sensibly?) and the performance (is it working with you the way you need?).\nThen loop: describe, check, refine. Treat a first draft as a starting point.\nGive specific feedback (\"cut the first two paragraphs and end with the action\"), not just \"make it better\".\nIf a chat goes off track, start a new chat with a clearer prompt.\nFor anything that matters, check the facts yourself and ask Claude to cite its source.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/getting-better-results","label":"Claude Academy: Getting better results"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/the-description-discernment-loop","label":"Claude Academy: The Description-Discernment loop"}]},
      {"h":"Useful follow-ups","prompt":"How confident are you in each part of this answer? List anything I should double-check, and where I would check it."},
      {"h":"A. spot the guess","text":"An AI tool was given the outage note and asked for a social media post. Find every statement that is not in the note, before you use the AI."},
      {"h":"The AI's post","text":"We know being without power is hard, and our crews are working around the clock to get you reconnected. Customers in [Parish A] can expect power back by Tuesday evening, and all of [Parish B] will be restored by Wednesday. As a thank-you for your patience, affected customers will receive a credit on their next bill. Please treat every fallen line as live and report it to [Hotline]."},
      {"h":"Step 2: paste the note and the post into Claude, then send","prompt":"Check the post above against the internal note only. List every statement in the post that the note does not support, and say what the note actually says. Do not use any outside knowledge."},
      {"h":"B. Data rules","text":"Part A without the AI: sort each item into Share, Anonymise first or Never share. Part B: rewrite the complaint so it could go into an AI tool and still do its job. Keep your cleaned version for Exercise 2."},
      {"h":"Part A: sort these","text":"The internal outage note · A customer's complaint email · A customer's account number · A screenshot of a customer's bill · Survey comments with no names · A customer's payment card details · The weekly complaints spreadsheet with case IDs only · A call recording"},
      {"h":"Part B: complaint (made up)","text":"From: d.campbell@example.com · Account 1234-5678-90 · Meter M-445566. I'm Donna Campbell of 22 Sample Lane, [Community]. My bill this month is $18,400, nearly double last month, and nobody has read my meter since the storm. My mother lives with me and uses an oxygen machine, so I can't afford to be disconnected. Call me on 876-555-0198."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-m4", time: "11:00", title: "Module 4: Projects: build the CX Outage Desk", minutes: 15, intro: "Set up a Claude Project once and every reply this week starts from the same facts and tone. In Claude: Projects, then Create project, named CX Outage Desk. Add practice/CX_Outage_Note.txt, then paste the instructions below. Exercises 1 to 4 run inside this desk.", step: "Claude 101, lesson 5",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"A Project is a workspace for ongoing work: its instructions and files apply to every chat inside it, so you never re-explain.\nUse one when you keep re-uploading the same documents, or when answers must always follow the same rules.\nName files clearly: Claude uses file names to find the right information.\nOn company plans you can share a Project with colleagues (view or edit), so the team works from one source.\nProjects store knowledge. Skills, in Module 6, store a way of doing a task.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/introduction-to-projects","label":"Claude Academy: Introduction to projects"}]},
      {"h":"Project instructions","prompt":"You are a customer care assistant at a Jamaican electricity company. Use only the facts in this project's documents. Never give a restoration time, amount, credit or date that is not in them; say \"we don't have that yet\" instead. Every message about outages includes: treat every fallen line as live and report it to [Hotline]. Tone: calm, warm, plain words, one apology at most, always a clear next step. Never include account numbers, names, addresses or phone numbers."},
      {"h":"Test 1 (new chat in the project)","prompt":"When will [Parish B] be back on?"},
      {"h":"Test 2","prompt":"Will customers get a credit?"}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-ex1", time: "11:15", title: "1. Service update for every channel (Monday)", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Customers need the same clear facts on every channel, and agents need a script.", step: "Working in your Project",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Prompt","prompt":"Role: You are a customer communications officer at an electricity utility in Jamaica.\nTask: Turn the internal outage note below into customer messages.\nContext: [the outage note is in your Outage Desk; in a normal chat, paste it here]\nFormat: 1) An SMS under 160 characters. 2) A social media post under 60 words. 3) A call script for agents: a 2-sentence opening, then short answers to \"When will my power be back?\" for [Parish A] and for [Parish B], and \"What do I do about a fallen line?\"\nCheck: Use only facts in the note. Never give a time the note does not give. Every version must include the fallen-line safety message. Plain words: no \"feeders\", \"restoration\" or other jargon."},
      {"h":"Follow-up (same chat)","prompt":"It is now Tuesday 6:00 p.m. Update: [Parish A] main areas are back on; some streets remain off because of local line damage; [Parish B] now expected by Thursday evening. Rewrite the SMS and the social post with this update."},
      {"h":"Builder","text":"Ask for the social post in Jamaican Patois, then ask a fluent speaker in the room to judge it. Would you publish it?"}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-ex2", time: "11:35", title: "2. The contact surge (Tuesday)", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Three jobs, about ten minutes each.", step: "Summarising and sorting",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Part A: rough call notes","text":"cust in [Parish B], power out since sat night. asked when back – told no estimate yet per mon update. reports fallen line on [Street] near school – logged hazard ref [REF], told keep away + keep kids away. elderly person w/ medical equip at home – flagged priority. promised call back after next update."},
      {"h":"Part A prompt","prompt":"Role: You are a contact centre agent at an electricity utility.\nTask: Turn my rough call notes into (1) a case note with the headings Issue, Actions taken, Outcome and Next step, and (2) a follow-up SMS to the customer under 50 words.\nContext: [paste the notes]\nFormat: Case note in short bullet points; SMS in plain words.\nCheck: Keep every fact, including the hazard reference, the priority flag and the callback. Add nothing that is not in the notes. Use [Customer] and [REF] as placeholders."},
      {"h":"Part B: reply to your cleaned complaint","prompt":"Role: You are a senior customer care agent at an electricity utility.\nTask: Write an email reply to the complaint below.\nContext: [paste your cleaned complaint]. What we know: meter reading is suspended this week because of the storm; some bills are estimated and will be adjusted after the next actual reading.\nFormat: Under 140 words. Acknowledge the frustration once, explain estimated billing in one plain sentence, then give the next steps.\nCheck: Do not promise anything not listed above. If the customer asks something we have not answered, such as about disconnection, say the agent will confirm and call back. Do not invent a date."},
      {"h":"Part C: a blunt reply","text":"As we posted already, [Parish B] has no time yet. Calling again won't make it faster. Check our page."},
      {"h":"Part C prompt","prompt":"Rewrite this reply so it is friendly and professional, keeps every fact, and is under 60 words. Then give a version under 30 words for WhatsApp."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-ex3", time: "11:55", title: "3. Escalation (Thursday)", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. One case has gone wrong, and the supervisor has 30 seconds before calling the customer.", step: "Summaries for decisions",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Case history (made up)","text":"Customer in [Parish B], priority flag (vulnerable person at home). Called Sunday, Monday, Tuesday and Wednesday. Monday: told no estimate yet. Tuesday: an agent told them power would be back \"by Wednesday\". That was not in any update. Thursday morning: still off, while neighbours across the road have power. Customer posted about it on social media last night. Asking for a supervisor to call, an explanation of why the neighbours have power, and compensation for spoiled food. Field team says the street has local line damage; repair scheduled, no time confirmed."},
      {"h":"Part A: summary for the supervisor","prompt":"Role: You are a senior contact centre agent.\nTask: Summarise this case for a supervisor who will call the customer in 5 minutes.\nContext: [paste the case history]\nFormat: Exactly 5 bullets: what happened, what went wrong on our side, what the customer wants, the risk if it is not resolved, the decision the supervisor needs to make.\nCheck: Neutral tone; no blame on the customer or a named colleague. Add no facts."},
      {"h":"Part B: an agent's draft reply","text":"Dear [Customer], thank you for your patience. Your street has local line damage, which is why some neighbours have power and you do not. Our crew is scheduled to repair it, and we will contact you as soon as we have a time. Regards, [Agent]"},
      {"h":"Part B prompt","prompt":"Here is a customer's case history and a draft reply. List every point or request the customer raised. For each, say whether the reply answers it: Yes, Partly or No. Then rewrite the reply to cover every point, without promising anything that is not in the case history."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-lunch", time: "12:15", title: "Lunch", minutes: 45, step: "", intro: "Back at 1:00 sharp.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "cx-m5", time: "1:00", title: "Module 5: Artifacts and files", minutes: 40, step: "Claude 101, lesson 6; built-in skills", intro: "Claude can make the finished thing, not just text in a chat. 5 minutes of teaching, then make two outputs from the morning's work and check them.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Artifacts are outputs that live in Claude: a doc, a deck, a design or a small tool. They open next to the chat, stay editable, and are shared by link. Ask for the deliverable: \"turn this into a one-page doc for the team\".\nClaude Docs, Claude Slides and Claude Design are the artifact types for documents, decks and visual designs, if JPS has them switched on.\nFiles are different: Claude makes a Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF file for you to download, using its built-in skills.\nEdit an artifact three ways: type in it yourself, comment for Claude, or ask in the chat.\nWhatever you make, open it and check it before it goes anywhere.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/creating-with-artifacts","label":"Claude Academy: Creating with artifacts"}]},
      {"h":"Option A: weekly report (Word)","prompt":"Using the outage note and what we produced this morning, create a Word report called \"Outage week: customer contact summary\" of no more than 2 pages, with the headings: What happened, What customers told us, Top themes, Actions for next week. Use only facts from the note and the files."},
      {"h":"Option B: complaints chart (Excel)","prompt":"Using the attached practice/CX_Complaints_Sample.xlsx, create an Excel file with a summary table of complaints by category and a bar chart of it. Keep the original data on its own sheet."},
      {"h":"Option C: management update (PowerPoint)","prompt":"Create a 3-slide PowerPoint for management: 1) the outage in numbers, using only the outage note, 2) what customers told us, 3) three recommended actions."},
      {"h":"Option D: a deck (Claude Slides, if switched on)","prompt":"Turn this morning's work into a five-slide deck for the management meeting: the outage in numbers, what customers told us, the top three themes, and three actions. Use only facts from the note and the files."},
      {"h":"Check every file","text":"Open it, don't just read Claude's reply. Tick each one:\n- Every number matches the note or the spreadsheet\n- No restoration time, credit or amount that isn't in the note\n- The chart's totals match the table\n- No names, account numbers or phone numbers"}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-m6", time: "1:40", title: "Module 6: Skills", minutes: 60, step: "Claude 101, lesson 7", intro: "A skill teaches Claude how to do one task your way, every time, in any chat. Projects store knowledge; skills store a way of working. Use one, read one, build one.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Skills are folders of instructions (and sometimes templates or scripts) that Claude loads when a task needs them.\nYou already use Anthropic's built-in skills whenever Claude makes a Word, Excel, PowerPoint or PDF file.\nThe easiest way to make your own is to ask Claude: it interviews you and builds the skill.\nThe description at the top of a skill is what tells Claude when to use it, so make it name the task plainly.\nOnly add skills from people you trust, and never put real names, accounts or cases in one.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/working-with-skills","label":"Claude Academy: Working with skills"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/tutorials/how-to-create-a-skill-with-claude-through-conversation","label":"Claude Academy: How to create a skill with Claude through conversation"}]},
      {"h":"1. Before and after (10 min): the outage update writer","text":"If your account owner has added it for everyone, find it under Customize, then Skills, and switch it on. If not, download the ZIP file below, open your skills (Customize, then Skills) and use Add to upload it.\n\nTest A: switch the skill OFF, start a new chat, and send the prompt below with practice/CX_Outage_Note.txt attached.\nTest B: switch the skill ON, start a new chat, and send exactly the same thing.\nCompare: did each version include the safety message, stick to the note, and show a fact check?","files":[{"href":"skills/outage-update-writer.zip","label":"Download outage-update-writer.zip"}]},
      {"h":"Test prompt (A and B)","prompt":"Write today's customer messages for the outage update."},
      {"h":"2. Look inside (10 min)","text":"Open the skill's instructions (link below). Find the four things every good skill has: a description saying when Claude should use it, the rules, the formats, and a check at the end. Which parts of a five-part prompt can you see? What would you add for your own team?","files":[{"href":"https://github.com/KennyCross/jps-ai-workshop/blob/main/skills/outage-update-writer/SKILL.md","label":"Open SKILL.md"}]},
      {"h":"3. Build your own (25 min, pairs)","text":"Pick a task your team does the same way every week. Ideas: case notes from rough call notes; WhatsApp replies to billing questions; an escalation summary for a supervisor.\n1. Send the prompt below and answer Claude's questions.\n2. Claude builds the skill and shows you the skill file. Use its save button to add it to your skills. (No button? Download the file, open your skills and use Add to upload it.)\n3. Test it in a new chat with a made-up example. Open the steps Claude took: one should load your skill. If it doesn't, ask Claude to make the skill's description more specific, then save the new version.\nMade-up examples only inside a skill: never real names, accounts or cases."},
      {"h":"Build prompt","prompt":"I want to create a skill for this task: [your task]. Interview me one question at a time: how the task should be done, what must always be included, what must never happen, and what a good result looks like. Then build the skill."},
      {"h":"4. Swap (5 min)","text":"Try a neighbouring pair's skill (share it from your skills, or pass the file) and suggest one improvement."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-break2", time: "2:40", title: "Break", minutes: 15, step: "", intro: "Back on time, please: the next block starts promptly.",
    parts: [

    ] },
  { id: "cx-m7", time: "2:55", title: "Module 7: Connectors, enterprise search and Research", minutes: 25, step: "Claude 101, lessons 8 to 10", intro: "Claude reaching beyond the chat: your work tools, your organisation's knowledge and the web. Teaching and demonstrations, then a quick routing game.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Key points","text":"Web search: a quick fact from current web pages, with links. On company plans an owner turns it on; in the newer Claude experience Claude searches when it helps.\nResearch: many searches that build on each other, then a cited report. Takes a few minutes or more. Start it from + in the message box.\nConnectors: Claude reaches tools such as Microsoft 365 (Outlook, SharePoint, OneDrive, Teams). It sees only what you can see. An owner adds a connector for the organisation, then you sign in with your own account.\nEnterprise search: answers from your organisation's own documents, chats and email, where it is set up.\nThinking: for a hard problem that needs reasoning, not information, choose a higher effort (click the model name, then Effort).","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/connecting-your-tools","label":"Claude Academy: Connecting your tools"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101/research-mode-for-deep-dives","label":"Claude Academy: Research for deep dives"}]},
      {"h":"Game: which tool? (10 min)","text":"For each request, choose: web search, Research, a connector or enterprise search, thinking (higher effort), or a Project.\n1. \"How many characters fit in one SMS?\"\n2. \"Compare how Caribbean utilities communicate with customers during outages, with sources.\"\n3. \"What did the team lead say about the reconnection backlog in yesterday's Teams chat?\"\n4. \"Work out the best order to call back 30 customers given these priorities.\"\n5. \"Answer customer questions using only this week's outage notes.\""},
      {"h":"Try web search (if it is on)","prompt":"What safety advice do electricity utilities give about fallen power lines after a storm? Give me the source links."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-m8", time: "3:20", title: "Module 8: Delegation and Diligence: Pilot Lab", minutes: 45, step: "AI Fluency: Delegation and Diligence", intro: "Decide what Claude should do in one real weekly task, build it, test it, and take responsibility for it. Pitches start at 4:05.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Delegation","text":"Before you build, split the work: what does Claude do, what do you do, and what do you do together?\nThree ways to work with Claude: automation (Claude does a defined task), augmentation (you think and create together), agency (Claude works on its own within limits you set).\nDelegate the drafting, sorting and summarising. Keep the decisions about people, money and promises."},
      {"h":"Diligence","text":"Creation: use the company account and approved tools, and only data you are allowed to share.\nTransparency: tell people when Claude played a significant part in work that affects them.\nDeployment: you are responsible for what you send. Check it as if you wrote it yourself.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations/a-closer-look-at-diligence","label":"Claude Academy: A closer look at Diligence"}]},
      {"h":"Chat, Project or skill?","text":"Chat: a one-off question or draft.\nProject: ongoing work that needs the same files and instructions, such as a policy desk.\nSkill: a task done the same way again and again, in any chat, such as a standard letter or update."},
      {"h":"Pilot plan (copy it and fill it in)","prompt":"PILOT PLAN\nTask:\nHow often we do it:\nTime it takes now:\nWe built a: Project / Skill, because:\nWhat Claude does:\nWhat a person always checks before it is used:\nData: what goes in, and what never goes in:\nHow we will know it works (one measure):\nWho needs to agree before we start:"},
      {"h":"Stress-test it","prompt":"Here are the instructions for my [Project / skill]: [paste them]. Act as a critical colleague. Give me 3 ways it could produce a wrong or risky answer, and a change to the instructions that would prevent each one."},
      {"h":"Diligence statement for your pilot","prompt":"Help me write a short diligence statement for this pilot: which AI tool we use, what Claude does in the task, what a person always checks before the work is used, and that we take responsibility for the final result."}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-pitch", time: "4:05", title: "Pilot pitches", minutes: 20, step: "Show what you built", intro: "Two minutes per pair, with what you built on the projector. Everyone votes for 2 pilots that aren't their own: the most useful, safe and ready to start on Monday. The top 3 go to management.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Your 2 minutes","text":"1. The task, and how often you do it\n2. What you built: show it working on a made-up example\n3. Before and after: time, quality, consistency\n4. What a person always checks\n5. How you will know it works"}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-close", time: "4:25", title: "Close", minutes: 5, step: "", intro: "Your Projects and skills stay in your Claude account. Write your commitment, fill in the feedback form, and keep learning on Claude Academy: the courses are free and end with a certificate.",
    parts: [
      {"h":"My commitment","prompt":"On Monday I will use Claude to ______, and before I use the answer I will check ______."},
      {"h":"Keep learning","text":"Claude 101: everything from today, in more depth.\nAI Fluency: Framework and foundations: the 4D framework.\nAI capabilities and limitations: why Claude gets things right and wrong.","files":[{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/claude-101","label":"Claude Academy: Claude 101"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-fluency-framework-foundations","label":"Claude Academy: AI Fluency"},{"href":"https://academy.claude.com/courses/ai-capabilities-and-limitations","label":"Claude Academy: AI capabilities and limitations"}]}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-ex4", time: "Extra", title: "Extra: 4. Difficult-call practice", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. One partner is the agent; Claude plays the caller; the other partner scores with the rubric. Use voice mode in the Claude phone app if you can (headphones on), otherwise type. About 8 exchanges, then END CALL. Swap and go again. Paste the outage note first.", step: "For fast pairs, or practice after the workshop",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Role-play prompt (new chat)","prompt":"You are Mr. Clarke, a customer of an electricity utility, calling the contact centre. You live in [Parish B]. Your power has been out since Saturday night; it is now Tuesday morning and you have heard nothing since Monday. Your food has spoiled. You are angry and you interrupt. Use only the outage note I gave you as the truth about the outage.\n\nHow to behave: if the agent acknowledges how you feel and gives honest information, calm down gradually. If the agent guesses a restoration time, blames the weather in a dismissive way, or reads a script without listening, get angrier. At some point demand compensation for your food. Keep each reply to 1 to 3 sentences, like a real caller.\n\nStay in character until I type END CALL. Then step out of character and score the agent from 1 to 3 on each of these: 1) Acknowledged the customer's feelings. 2) Gave only accurate information. 3) Made no promises outside their authority. 4) Gave a clear next step. 5) Kept a calm, respectful tone. Quote one line from the agent as evidence for each score, and give one thing to do differently next time."},
      {"h":"Rubric (1 to 3 each)","text":"Acknowledge: 1 skips to facts · 2 a set phrase · 3 names the customer's actual problem\nAccurate: 1 guesses a time or cause · 2 one vague claim · 3 only what the update says\nPromises: 1 promises compensation or a time · 2 hints at one · 3 explains who decides and when\nNext step: 1 none · 2 vague · 3 specific: what happens, who calls, when\nTone: 1 defensive or rushed · 2 polite but scripted · 3 calm, warm, steady under interruption"}
    ] },
  { id: "cx-ex5", time: "Extra", title: "Extra: 5. The week's report (Friday)", minutes: 20, intro: "Write first: before you open the prompts below, spend 3 minutes writing your own five-part prompt (Role, Task, Context, Format, Check) and run it. Then run ours and compare the two answers. Pairs do Part A or Part B, then swap findings. Attach the practice spreadsheets.", step: "For fast pairs, or practice after the workshop",
    parts: [
      {"h":"Part A: feedback themes (practice/CX_Feedback_Comments.xlsx)","prompt":"Role: You are a customer insights analyst.\nTask: Group the 30 comments in the attached file into no more than 6 themes.\nContext: The file holds survey comments with a 1 to 5 rating (made up).\nFormat: A table with Theme, Number of comments, Average rating and one example in your own words. Then the two themes management should act on first, and why.\nCheck: Put every comment in exactly one theme. Show that the counts add up to 30. List the response IDs in each theme so I can check."},
      {"h":"Part B: complaints update (practice/CX_Complaints_Sample.xlsx)","prompt":"Role: You are a CX reporting analyst.\nTask: Using the attached complaints spreadsheet (made up): 1) count complaints by category and by channel; 2) give the average days to resolve for each category and the number still open; 3) write a management update under 180 words: what stands out, what needs attention, one recommendation.\nFormat: Small tables for the figures, then the update.\nCheck: Show how you calculated each figure so I can check it in Excel. Average days to resolve uses resolved cases only."},
      {"h":"Then make the files (same chat)","prompt":"Create an Excel file with the category table and a bar chart, and a Word document with the management update."}
    ] }
  ]
}
};
